# GETPASS(3)

## NAME

getpass - read a password

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <unistd.h>

char *getpass(const char *prompt);
```

## DESCRIPTION

*Getpass* reads a password from the file */dev/tty*, or if that cannot be opened, from the
standard input, after prompting with the null-terminated string *prompt* and disabling echoing.
A pointer is returned to a null-terminated string of at most 8 characters.

**Note:** the prompt goes out through fputs(3) here. v7 passed the caller's string to
fprintf(3) as a *format* - harmless in 1979, and a hole worth closing.

## FILES

| /dev/tty
| share/besm6/lib/libc.a, lib/libc/stdio/getpass.c

## SEE ALSO

crypt(3), getpwent(3)

## BUGS

The return value points to static data whose content is overwritten by each call.

**There is no test for this routine,** and there cannot easily be one: it opens /dev/tty and
would sit there waiting for someone to type, which a harness that diffs a program's output
against a recorded expectation cannot arrange. That is also why the bug below went unnoticed
for as long as it did.

## BESM-6 NOTES

### The bound on the copy is an index, and that was not tidying up

v7 wrote

for (p = pbuf; ...) if (p < &pbuf[8]) \*p++ = c;

and `<` **between two char \* values does not order them on this machine.** A fat pointer
carries its byte offset in bits 47..45 and its word address in bits 15..1, and **the offset
decrements as the pointer advances;** there is no relational helper for the general case, so
the comparison reduces to an integer comparison of the whole word and the offset field
dominates the address field.

*Pbuf* is nine characters, so *p* starts at byte #0 of its first word (offset field 5) while
**&pbuf[8]** is byte #2 of the second (offset field 3). **The test was false on the very first
iteration:** nothing was ever stored, and *getpass* returned the empty string *every time.*

It is **if (n < 8) pbuf[n++] = c** now. See the same hazard in libtermcap and libcurses, whose
ports had four and eleven of these to remove.

### Dispositions are void (\*)(int)

The signal it saves and restores across the read is stored in that type, which is what
<signal.h> declares and what the kernel's frame delivers. v7 wrote **int (\*)()**.
